### What version of the Codex App are you using (From “About Codex” dialog)?

26.928.20755 (build 12246), installed as ChatGPT.app / bundle ID com.openai.codex. Bundled Codex CLI: 0.159.0.

### What subscription do you have?

Not collected for this report. The parser-level reproduction below does not require an account.

### What platform is your computer?

macOS 26.5.1, Apple Silicon. `uname -mprs`: `Darwin 25.5.0 arm64 arm`. Standalone reproduction runtime: bundled Node.js v24.21.0.

### What issue are you seeing?

Opening one existing local Work chat created by a Chat-to-Work handoff repeatedly freezes the entire desktop UI. Restarting the application and backing up/clearing approximately 487 MiB of rebuildable UI caches did not resolve it.

The first user message contains an unfenced, serialized reference to the source ChatGPT conversation, including arrays and many embedded code fences. The entire message is only 8,415 characters; the serialized reference is one 4,957-character line. The projected chat contains only six items.

Observed on the real failing UI:
- One renderer was at approximately 102% CPU. All 435 main-thread samples during a five-second native sample remained in a JavaScript/JIT call chain.
- Backend history reads completed in milliseconds; resuming the chat took approximately 239–246 ms in repeated logs.
- Reopening the same chat reproduced the freeze after restarts and cache cleanup.

Isolated replay of the exact JavaScript shipped in the signed application narrows the failure to the Marked inline-link regex, rather than network or backend task execution:
- The original first message exceeded an 8-second timeout in the shipped lexer.
- The other five item texts parsed in approximately 4–11 ms.
- Instrumenting RegExp.exec immediately before each call identified the inline-link regex below as the call that did not return within the probe timeout.
- Replacing the actual words with placeholders preserved the failure. A completely synthetic 81-character input also exceeds a two-second timeout in the same regex.

### What steps can reproduce the bug?

**Observed UI sequence:** hand off a Chat conversation containing multiple embedded code blocks to local Work, with the prior conversation included as a single unfenced serialized reference; open the resulting Work chat. The original private message is not attached. The exact UI sequence was reproduced with that existing chat; a new synthetic end-to-end handoff has not been tested.

**Independent, privacy-free parser reproduction:** save the following as `codex-markdown-repro.mjs` and run `node codex-markdown-repro.mjs`. It uses the inline-link regex copied from this installed build and generated text only. Every case runs in a disposable Node worker with a two-second timeout. Do not paste the expanded pathological string into an important chat.

```js
// Extracted inline-link regex from installed desktop 26.928.20755.
// All test input is generated; no conversation data or dependencies.
import {Worker, isMainThread, parentPort, workerData} from 'node:worker_threads';
const regexSource = "^!?\\[((?:\\[(?:\\\\[\\s\\S]|[^\\[\\]\\\\])*\\]|\\\\[\\s\\S]|`+[^`]*?`+(?!`)|[^\\[\\]\\\\`])*?)\\]\\(\\s*(<(?:\\\\.|[^\\n<>\\\\])+>|[^ \\t\\n\\x00-\\x1f]*)(?:(?:[ \\t]*(?:\\n[ \\t]*)?)(\"(?:\\\\\"?|[^\"\\\\])*\"|'(?:\\\\'?|[^'\\\\])*'|\\((?:\\\\\\)?|[^)\\\\])*\\)))?\\s*\\)";
if (isMainThread) {
  for (const groups of [4, 8, 12, 16, 20]) {
    const result = await new Promise((resolve, reject) => {
      const worker = new Worker(new URL(import.meta.url), {workerData: {groups}});
      let settled = false;
      const timer = setTimeout(async () => {
        if (settled) return;
        settled = true;
        await worker.terminate();
        resolve({groups, chars: 1 + groups * 4, timeoutMs: 2000});
      }, 2000);
      worker.once('message', async (message) => {
        if (settled) return;
        settled = true;
        clearTimeout(timer);
        await worker.terminate();
        resolve(message);
      });
      worker.once('error', error => { if (!settled) {settled = true; clearTimeout(timer); reject(error);} });
    });
    console.log(JSON.stringify(result));
  }
} else {
  const input = '[' + (String.fromCharCode(96).repeat(3) + 'x').repeat(workerData.groups);
  const start = performance.now();
  const match = new RegExp(regexSource).exec(input);
  parentPort.postMessage({groups: workerData.groups, chars: input.length, ms: performance.now() - start, matched: match !== null});
}
```

Observed regex-only timings on the runtime above:

| Repeated groups | Input characters | Result |
| --- | ---: | --- |
| 4 | 17 | 0.08 ms, no match |
| 8 | 33 | 0.15 ms, no match |
| 12 | 49 | 2.85 ms, no match |
| 16 | 65 | 93.8 ms, no match |
| 20 | 81 | Stopped at 2,000 ms |

These are local diagnostic measurements, not standardized cross-machine benchmarks. The full shipped lexer showed the same scaling and exceeded two seconds on the 81-character case.

### What is the expected behavior?

Rendering a stored message must perform bounded work and keep the UI responsive, including when the text is malformed Markdown or serialized conversation data. This short synthetic input should be handled promptly.

### Additional information

**Hotspot/provenance:** the bundled shared module is `webview/assets/app-shared-5d8e744d1fa1.js`; the user-message extension is `user-message-markdown-92bfdf953148.js`. The inline-link pattern has a repeated label alternative containing `` `+[^`]*?`+(?!`) ``. The incomplete bracketed synthetic input with repeated backtick runs causes severe backtracking when a valid link ending cannot be found.

The native stack is not a symbolized JavaScript stack. Attribution to this regex comes from the isolated exact-bundle replay and the subsequent targeted display workaround. The tests did not determine the first affected version or compare other app releases. No unbounded/infinite duration is claimed beyond the recorded timeouts.

**Controlled recovery:** wrapping only the serialized reference line in a four-backtick plain-text fenced block made the message parse in approximately 8 ms in an isolated replay with the shipped user-message extensions (a minimal DOM entity-decoder stub was used for module initialization). The original reference text and the remainder of the user's request were preserved.

After backup and explicit user approval, that formatting-only change was applied to one local display-projection record while the app was closed. The original rollout/transcript and signed app bundle were untouched. The user reopened the affected chat and confirmed it worked. The persisted message parsed in approximately 14 ms after restart. The two renderers were then at approximately 7.9% and 1.1% CPU; the busier renderer spent 319/429 main-thread samples waiting for messages instead of continuously executing JavaScript.

This was a reversible diagnostic workaround, not a proposed supported database-editing procedure or a fix to the shipped parser.

**Suggested investigation:** remove the ambiguous/backtracking behavior in the inline-link tokenizer; render serialized handoff references as plain text or structured data; use an off-main-thread parsing boundary with a real timeout and plain-text fallback. A length cap alone is insufficient for the 81-character example.

**Related, not confirmed duplicates:** #25865 describes a different paste-time link-detection regex with escaped backslashes; #34454 describes a handoff/internal-link-related freeze on Windows without this regex attribution. This report concerns persisted user-message rendering and includes a backtick-based reproducer requiring no URL.

Only synthetic input and aggregate timings are included. No original conversation text, thread identifiers, account details, personal filesystem paths, raw logs, or database files are attached. Please route to the desktop message-rendering / Chat-to-Work handoff team.

